# Scenario: the same raw-socket exchange machinery, driven over TLS.
#
# The T-1 client leg (#198) shipped with unit coverage and no scenario: nothing
# in the tree pointed it at a real nginx TLS listener, so every claim about it
# rested on http_test.c's throwaway servers. This scenario is the other half.
# It asserts nothing about PKI -- the certificate is a self-signed fixture and
# the client does not verify it (see http.h on http_tls.verify). What it
# asserts is that a request written into a TLS transport reaches an nginx
# server, that the response comes back intact, and that the connection and
# descriptor accounting behave exactly as they do on the plaintext path.
#
# A NOTE ON WHAT MAKES THIS NON-VACUOUS. Every case below would also pass over
# plaintext against an ordinary listener, so the assertions alone do not prove
# TLS happened. What proves it is the LISTENER: nginx.conf carries `listen ...
# ssl`, so a client that did not perform a handshake gets its plaintext bytes
# rejected and every case here fails. The negative control is structural, and
# it is checked by the last case in this file.

# Baseline. If the handshake itself is broken this is where it shows, before
# any case that could be blamed on framing or on the reader.
name    a request over TLS reaches the server
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~OK
delta   fds == 0

# The body has to survive reassembly across TLS records. The case above fits in
# one record and one read, so it cannot distinguish a client that reads exactly
# one record from one that reads to the end of the response. This one can: the
# assertion is on the TAIL of the body, which a client that stopped at the
# first record never sees.
name    a response longer than one TLS record arrives whole
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /longer HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~vwxyz
delta   fds == 0

# The teardown path. http_close() drops the fd from the SSL* side table, and a
# leak there is invisible to a single request -- it shows as a descriptor that
# is never returned. Asserting fds == 0 after a second, independent TLS
# connection is what makes the side-table teardown observable from outside the
# client.
name    a second TLS connection leaves no descriptor behind
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds == 0

# THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, and the reason the rest of this file is evidence about
# TLS rather than evidence about HTTP.
#
# `probe_baseline` subtracts from the run's ORIGIN snapshot, so it sees the
# whole batch above at once: an SSL* or a descriptor leaked anywhere in it
# fails here and nowhere else, because each per-case `delta` compares snapshots
# that already carry the leak.
#
# The failure mode this scenario is most exposed to is PROBER_TLS ceasing to
# reach the prober -- an env file rename, the ${PROBER_TLS:+--tls} expansion
# dropped from run-scenario.sh. The client then sends plaintext at an `ssl`
# listener, which cannot answer it, so the scenario CANNOT silently degrade
# into a plaintext test. Measured 2026-08-19 by commenting out the export: the
# run exits 2 and the suite goes red.
#
# BUT NOT VIA A `not ok` LINE, and that is worth knowing before trusting a TAP
# parser here. `probe_baseline` needs an ORIGIN snapshot, taken before the
# plan is printed; the plaintext probe fetch fails, and prober.c die()s at that
# point -- which is above the `printf("1..%zu")`. The stream is therefore
# EMPTY: no plan, no ok, no not ok, just the diagnostic on stderr and exit 2.
# A consumer gating on the exit status sees the failure; one parsing TAP alone
# sees a scenario that emitted nothing. That ordering is pre-existing and
# affects every scenario using probe_baseline, not just this one.
name    no descriptor drift across the whole TLS batch
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds == 0
probe_baseline  fds == 0
